<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Caroline of Zweibrücken</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1721">1721</a>–<a href="/years/1774">1774</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Great Landgravine, Countess Palatine Caroline of Zweibrücken</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Caroline of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zweibrucken">Zweibrücken</a>, known as the Great Landgravine, was the wife of Landgrave Louis IX of Hesse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darmstadt">Darmstadt</a>. While her husband stayed mostly at his garrison town of Pirmasens, she managed the court at Darmstadt and made it a meeting place for writers of the age of sensibility, including Goethe, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-gottfried-herder">Herder</a> and Johann Heinrich Merck. She arranged prominent marriages for her daughters, one of whom married the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-ii-of-prussia">Frederick William II of Prussia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/caroline-of-zweibrucken.jpg" alt="Caroline of Zweibrücken" width="320" height="412" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caroline_of_Zweibr%C3%BCcken_-_Badisches_Landesmuseum.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
